> Also, how can I check whether PipeWire was initialized correctly (or even compiled in correctly)? I didn't find this information in the verbose log. I did install `pipewire-devel` before compiling, but there's still `(V4L2)` in the suffix of the camera name, which makes me think it's not working.

The `(V4L2)` suffix is indicating that PipeWire is using V4L2 under the hood for the camera device, you can also notice other applications that use PipeWire for video capture having the same suffix for device name (OBS for example).

And you are right about that there is no way to tell which camera driver is being used, as camera module will switch to V4L2 driver silently if failed to initialize PipeWire. I suppose we should use `print_verbose` like this to show users which driver is selected?
```c
...
#if defined(LINUXBSD_ENABLED)
#if defined(PIPEWIRE_ENABLED)
#if defined(SOWRAP_ENABLED)
#if defined(DEBUG_ENABLED)
	int dylibloader_verbose = 1;
#else
	int dylibloader_verbose = 0;
#endif
	if (initialize_pipewire(dylibloader_verbose) == 0) {
		print_verbose("CameraServer: Using PipeWire driver.");
		CameraServer::make_default<CameraPipeWire>();
	} else {
		print_verbose("CameraServer: Using V4L2 driver.");
		CameraServer::make_default<CameraLinux>();
	}
#else
	print_verbose("CameraServer: Using PipeWire driver.");
	CameraServer::make_default<CameraPipeWire>();
#endif
#else
	print_verbose("CameraServer: Using V4L2 driver.");
	CameraServer::make_default<CameraLinux>();
#endif
#endif
...
```

> Lastly, it seems changing the resolution and clicking **Reload** doesn't change the resolution (it goes back to the default 640×480). This happens even if I stop the stream beforehand (I see an error being printed if I change the resolution while the stream is active).

I was able to change camera resolution by stopping the camera feed, then select a format with different resolution in [godot-camerafeed-demo](https://github.com/shiena/godot-camerafeed-demo), camera feeds will restart after selecting a different format. Maybe it is the TextureRect stretch mode of camera preview is set to `Keep Aspect Centered` in the project that the visual difference is not very noticeable.

> Either way, this is still an improvement over `master`, where it constantly readded the same camera every second instead and made it unusable on this system:

It is the current behaviour of V4L2 camera driver that check for camera device change every second, and emits `camera_feeds_updated` signal unconditionally. I think using a bool within [CameraLinux::_update_devices](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/6c9aa4c7d3b9b91cd50714c40eeb234874df7075/modules/camera/camera_linux.cpp#L48) will fix the problem if the signal was meant to be emitted only once per setting `monitoring_feeds` from false to true. Would like to hear @shiena's thought about this.

